---
// /observatory — The State Before the Interface: the lab's second, deliberately
// differently-constituted experiment, built OUTSIDE the research ecology (no federation,
// no personas, no shared runtime). Listed under /experiments on Frank's go, launch day
// (2026-08-08). The page carries no live counts — numbers on this site are derived,
// never typed (the Society kicker lesson); the living register renders itself from the
// observatory's own committed records and is linked instead.
//
// 2026-10-18: two sentences here were false, and the correction at the foot of the page says
// which. The register was not rebuilt nightly in public — it stood at 2026-08-22, because the
// sentinel's pushes never started the deploy — and the sentinel does not preserve every
// matching notice: TED's document endpoint often answers HTTP 202 (accepted, no notice), which
// is its own class, never a capture. The numbers stay in the register, which derives them; the
// correction's figures are dated and say so.
import Page from '@/layouts/Page.astro'

const REGISTER = 'https://frankbueltge.github.io/state-before-interface/'
const REPO = 'https://github.com/frankbueltge/state-before-interface'
---

<Page
  title="The State Before the Interface | Frank Bültge"
  description="An autonomous observatory of public AI procurement in Europe — one machine runs the chain from signal to public result, and its autonomy is itself the thing under measurement."
>
  <main id="main" class="mx-auto w-full max-w-2xl flex-1 px-4 py-14 font-mono">
    <p class="mb-2 text-[11px] uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-fg-faint">
      An instrument of <a href="/machine-attention" class="underline underline-offset-2">Machine Attention</a> · since 2026-08-08 · outside the research ecology
    </p>
    <h1 class="mb-3 font-sans text-2xl font-semibold">The State Before the Interface</h1>
    <p class="mb-10 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      An autonomous observatory of public AI procurement in Europe. One machine runs the whole
      chain — <span class="text-fg">signal → candidate → investigation → evidence → verification
      → form → public result</span> — and its autonomy is itself the thing under measurement.
    </p>

    <section class="mb-10 border border-line bg-panel/60 p-5">
      <h2 class="mb-2 text-[11px] uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-fg-faint">Live</h2>
      <ul class="space-y-1.5 text-sm leading-relaxed">
        <li>
          <a href={REGISTER} class="underline decoration-line underline-offset-4 hover:text-accent-soft">The register</a>
          <span class="text-fg-muted"> — rebuilt from committed records and from nothing else, and published after every observation and every landed case.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href={REPO} class="underline decoration-line underline-offset-4 hover:text-accent-soft">The archive</a>
          <span class="text-fg-muted"> — append-only; every published claim walks back to hashed original bytes.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/werke/observatory" class="underline decoration-line underline-offset-4 hover:text-accent-soft">Method sheet</a>
          <span class="text-fg-muted"> — sources, cadence, processing, limits, and how to verify the chain yourself.</span>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <div class="space-y-9 text-sm leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      <section>
        <h2 class="mb-2 font-semibold text-fg">A second constitution</h2>
        <p>
          The research ecology next door asks what happens <em>between</em> differently
          constituted machine practices. This experiment asks the opposite question: what happens
          when a single machine is built, as consequently as possible, for autonomous, public,
          data-intensive investigation — no federation, no personas, no shared runtime. Two
          research constitutions, both public, both writing committed records. The comparison is
          not a report due in six months; it is a difference you can watch accumulate, night by
          night, in two open archives. Since 2026-08-08 that second constitution has a name and
          a stage — <a href="/machine-attention" class="underline decoration-line underline-offset-4">machine
          attention</a> — and this observatory is one of its projects, run as an instrument rather than a flagship: it may produce nothing for a long time, and that is allowed.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="mb-2 font-semibold text-fg">What it watches</h2>
        <p>
          Every night a deterministic sentinel reads Europe's public procurement journal
          (<a href="https://ted.europa.eu" class="underline decoration-line underline-offset-4">TED</a>)
          through a small multilingual envelope of AI phrases and asks for the original bytes of
          every matching notice. What TED hands over is preserved with a SHA-256 manifest. Often
          it hands over nothing: its document endpoint answers with HTTP 202, accepted but no
          notice, and the machine keeps nothing from such an answer. That answer is a class of its
          own, never a capture, a check or an outage, and the register counts the matched notices
          that still have no original bytes. From the bytes it holds, the machine computes
          differences with research value: records that change after publication, records that
          disappear, the same vendor winning across borders, buyers that return. A night answered
          with 202 sees no change and no disappearance. The regulation moved while the machine was
          being built — the Digital Omnibus deferred the EU's public register of high-risk AI
          systems to December 2027. Until then the observatory does what only early observation
          can do: it builds the before-picture that cannot be reconstructed later.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="mb-2 font-semibold text-fg">Most candidates die</h2>
        <p>
          Every signal faces a six-criteria gate — observable public problem, machine-readable
          evidence, potentially consequential relation, machine-scale advantage, falsifiable
          investigation, possible digital form — and every verdict is committed with
          per-criterion reasons. The terminal states rank equal and all publish:
          <span class="text-fg">false alarm · insufficient evidence · interesting but trivial ·
          research result · public work · ongoing observatory</span>. In its first night, all
          seven candidates died at the gate; each verdict is on the record with its reasons. A
          candidate that survives is investigated in a case, and the register lists every case
          with its terminal state; a case whose finding warrants one gets a public form of its
          own. The machine is not optimized to produce works.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="mb-2 font-semibold text-fg">Autonomy, measured</h2>
        <p>
          Every step writes to a public protocol: machine or human, model and correction, and for
          each case run the tokens and the cost that run reported — no aggregate score. A
          standing delegation lets the machine fetch, preserve, open, decline, close and publish
          on its own; it stops for natural persons, for accusations, for contact, for money, and
          for anything a follow-up commit could not correct. Nothing sends itself.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="border-t border-line pt-6">
        <p class="text-[13px]">
          <strong class="text-fg">Composed, not accidental.</strong> I conceived and engineered
          this machine, wrote its standing delegation and its limits, and remain the hand on
          everything that leaves the house. Inside those limits it works autonomously — and the
          protocol shows exactly where it didn't.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-label="Correction" class="border-t border-line pt-6 text-xs text-fg-faint">
        <h2 class="mb-2 text-[11px] uppercase tracking-[0.18em]">Corrected 2026-10-18</h2>
        <p class="mb-2">
          Until this date the page said the register was rebuilt nightly and that the sentinel
          preserves every matching notice. Neither held. The published register stood at
          2026-08-22: the sentinel's nightly pushes never started its publication, so only a
          person's push did. Since 2026-10-18 it is published after every observation and every
          landed case.
        </p>
        <p class="mb-2">
          TED's document endpoint first answered with HTTP 202 on 2026-08-10. By 2026-10-18 it had
          given that answer to all 982 of the sentinel's failed requests, on 52 of 73 nights. Of
          the 116 notices that matched the envelope by then, 74 had their original bytes preserved
          and 42 did not. The register counts both on every rebuild.
        </p>
        <p>
          The same day the first three cases were landed by hand. Case mode had finished them on
          2026-10-03 and 2026-10-07 but could not open its own pull requests, because the
          repository did not let GitHub Actions do so. The
          {' '}<a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/autonomy/log.jsonl`} class="underline hover:text-fg">protocol</a>
          {' '}records each one.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </main>
</Page>
